<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Iberia</p><h1 class="st-title">Hispania Ulterior</h1><p class="st-meta">197 BCE – 27 BCE · capital Corduba</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Rome's province of Further Spain, covering the rich south and west of Iberia from 197 BCE until Augustus split it into Baetica and Lusitania.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After driving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> in 206 BCE, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> first governed the conquered south through commanders. In 197 BCE it created two provinces, each under a praetor; Hispania Ulterior covered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guadalquivir">Guadalquivir</a> valley and the lands to the west, including <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gadir">Gades</a> and the former territory of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/turdetani">Turdetani</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Governors based at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Corduba</a>, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-claudius-marcellus-consul-166-bce">Marcus Claudius Marcellus</a> in the 2nd century BCE, commanded armies and collected tribute from Iberian and Phoenician towns. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">Silver</a> mining, oil and grain made it rich, while campaigns against the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lusitanians">Lusitanians</a> dominated the 2nd century BCE. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> served there as quaestor and then governor in 61 BCE.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the civil wars it was a stronghold of the Pompeians, defeated by Caesar at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-munda">Munda</a> in 45 BCE. From 27 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augustus-roman-emperor">Augustus</a> reorganised it into <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/hispania-baetica">Hispania Baetica</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/lusitania">Lusitania</a>, though the formal division took several years.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="other, 197 BCE – 27 BCE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M26 24 H74 V52 Q74 74 50 84 Q26 74 26 52 Z"/><path d="M50 24 V84 M26 46 H74"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">other</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Iberia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:55.78%;width:3.38%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>197 BCE – 27 BCE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Corduba</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Servius Sulpicius Galba, Julius Caesar</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>, <a href="/countries/portugal">Portugal</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Further Spain</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-112&amp;focus=hispania-ulterior" aria-label="Hispania Ulterior on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4561 816 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Hispania Ulterior lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4881 1072L4842 1116M4881 1072L4975 1081M4881 1072L4931 1085M4881 1072L5304 942M4881 1072L4881 1072M4881 1072L4821 1043"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4842 1116h0M4975 1081h0M4931 1085h0M5304 942h0M4881 1072h0M4821 1043h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4881 1072h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4881 1072h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Corduba. <a href="/atlas?year=-112&amp;focus=hispania-ulterior">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
